module

public import FormalConjectures.ErdosProblems.«387»

/-!
# Erdős Problem 387, Schinzel variant (Colloq. Math. P 217): all sufficiently large `k`

Generated by an AI agent and machine-checked; not reviewed by a human expert.

## Statement

`main` has exactly the type of the upstream theorem `Erdos387.erdos_387.variants.schinzel` (Formal
Conjectures, `FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/387.lean`, commit 9097e2f, where it is marked
`category research open`):

    ∀ᶠ k in atTop, ¬ IsPrimePow k → ∃ n : ℕ, ∀ i < k, ¬ n - i ∣ n.choose k

In words: there is a `k0` such that for every `k ≥ k0` that is not a prime power there is an `n` for
which none of `n, n-1, ..., n-k+1` divides the binomial coefficient `C(n,k)`. There are no extra
hypotheses. The axioms are `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.

## Proof outline

For every prime `p ≤ k` choose a
class `a p ∈ (k % p, p]` such that every `j ∈ [1, k]` lies in one of these classes. By Kummer's
theorem (here Lucas's congruence is used instead) and the Chinese remainder theorem this gives `n`
(Lemma 1).

* Start with class zero for all primes. Move the largest prime factor `q` of `k` to class 1, and an
  auxiliary prime `p0` to a class `c0 > k % p0` which contains many primes `P ∈ (4k/5, k]`. Such a
  `p0` is found by a second moment argument and pigeonhole (Lemma 5; the construction gives
  `p0 ≤ (log k) ^ 6`, but only `p0 ≤ k` is stated and needed). What is not covered is composed of
  moved primes. The prime 2 is never moved, so these numbers are odd.
* The level of `n` is `min n (k - n)`. Cover the uncovered numbers in order of increasing level, up
  to level `k/5`. A number of level `l` is covered by a fresh prime `p`, which exceeds `l` and
  divides an even number `k - i` with `i < l`. By parity, everything that becomes uncovered has
  level at least `p > l` (Lemmas 9 and 16).
* Fresh primes are found among the prime factors of `C(⌊k/2⌋, h)`, `h ≈ l/2`, with Chebyshev's
  bounds (Lemma 7). At bounded levels they come from Størmer's theorem (Lemmas 2 and 8).
* There are few numbers to cover. At levels below a power of `log k`, Rankin's bound controls the
  numbers `n` themselves. The numbers `k - t` are controlled by the fact that one composed of few
  primes has a large prime power as a factor (Lemmas 10 to 12). At larger levels the prime for level
  `l` is at least the threshold `thr l`, which is about the smaller of `l log l` and `l log (k/l)`,
  up to a constant factor. A weight on levels is chosen such that all multiples of this prime
  together weigh at most half as much as the number of level `l` that it covers (Lemma 6). It
  follows that the total weight is small (Lemma 13).
* The few remaining numbers have level at least `k/5`. They are covered by different primes
  `P ∈ (4k/5, k]` in the class of `p0`. Moving such a prime uncovers only `P`, which the class of
  `p0` contains (Lemma 18).

## Scope

* This is a variant attached to Problem 387, not its headline question (a divisor of `C(n,k)` in
  `(cn, n]`), which was answered by Bui, Naprienko, Pratt and Zaharescu (cited below).
* With `H_k` the proposition "for every `n`, some `n - i` with `0 ≤ i < k` divides `C(n,k)`", P 217
  asks whether `H_k` holds for infinitely many `k ≠ p^a`, and Schinzel expected a negative answer.
  The theorem gives this answer. The threshold `k0` is not explicit. So nothing is proved about any
  specific `k`, and the explicit form (`H_k` fails for every `k ≥ 35`, `k ≠ p^a`) remains open as
  far as known.
* No defect is known in the upstream statement. It omits the usual condition `n ≥ 2k`, which is
  harmless: Schinzel notes that the proposition is true for `n < 2k`. He counts `1 = p^0` as a prime
  power and Mathlib does not, which is irrelevant for large `k`.

## Priority

New as far as known: no published or public proof of the all-large-`k` statement, informal or
formal, was found as of 25 Nov 2026, neither by the agents that reviewed the first version of this
proof nor by a second, independent prior-art search. This means that no proof was found, not that
none exists. The main devices of the proof are older; see "Credits for ideas".

* Limit on the claim: Erdős wrote in 1985 (Rocky Mountain J. Math. 15, pp. 358-359,
  https://users.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1985-27.pdf) that Lacampagne and Selfridge "found many such n in
  fact they probably can find such n for every k ≠ p^α". Read as in Mat. Lapok 1978/82, every such
  `n` is a counterexample to `H_k`. (Taken literally, the sentence conflicts with Schinzel's
  Corollary 2, since `H_6` is true.) No paper with a proof was found. It is not known what they had.
* No published theorem was found from which the statement follows quickly. Erdős states the
  conjecture as open in 1978/82 and 1985. Upstream and the other public trackers that were seen
  still do (until Oct 2026).
* Not seen: MathSciNet, Google Scholar, Guy B34, the list of unsolved problems in Schinzel's Selecta
  (2007), remarks on P 217 in Colloq. Math. later than R 1, and Erdős, Lacampagne and Selfridge,
  Math. Comp. 61 (1993) 215-224. Some searches were rate-limited. The Erdős problems site was not
  requested; a public copy of its discussion thread of 10 Jul 2026 was read.
* To be checked by an expert: the places just listed; any proof or manuscript of Lacampagne and
  Selfridge, and papers citing Erdős's remark on them; papers citing Schinzel 1958 or Erdős-Nicolas
  1981, in particular any that determines `f(t!)`; whether Bui, Naprienko, Pratt or Zaharescu have
  an unpublished cover for every large `k`; the discussion thread after 10 Jul 2026.
* If cited: "Schinzel's conjecture (Colloq. Math. P 217, 1958) in the eventual form".

## Earlier work on this statement (none of it proves it)

* A. Schinzel, "Sur un problème de P. Erdős", Colloq. Math. 5 (1958) 198-204,
  doi:10.4064/cm-5-2-198-204, https://matwbn.icm.edu.pl/ksiazki/cm/cm5/cm5128.pdf: `H_k` is true for
  prime powers; the reduction to a restricted cover; `H_k` fails for `k = 15, 21, 22, 33` and for no
  other `k ≤ 33`; Problems P 216 (does `H_k` fail infinitely often?) and P 217.
* P. Erdős, letter of 5 Feb 1957, printed there, pp. 203-204: `H_k` fails for infinitely many `k`.
* "P 217, R 1", Colloq. Math. 10 (1963) 365,
  https://www.impan.pl/shop/en/publication/transaction/download/product/95243: Schinzel proved
  `H_34`; E. Burbacka and J. Piekarczyk refuted `H_k` for all `k ≠ p^a` from 35 to 150.
* P. Erdős, "On prime factors of binomial coefficients II" (in Hungarian), Mat. Lapok (1978/82)
  307-316, https://users.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1982-14.pdf, and the paper of 1985 cited above: the
  conjecture, stated as open. (They print `n ≥ 34` and `k > 33`, which include `k = 34`; this looks
  like a slip for `k ≥ 35`.) Mat. Lapok p. 316 also reports a remark of Ruzsa:
  `min a_i > c k / log k` is possible, where `a_i` is the `k`-smooth part of `n - i`. As printed it
  does not ask that `C(n,k)` be free of primes `≤ k`, and without that it does not give
  `¬ n - i ∣ C(n,k)`.
* P. Erdős and J.-L. Nicolas, Ann. Fac. Sci. Toulouse 3 (1981) 173-199,
  http://www.numdam.org/item/AFST_1981_5_3_3-4_173_0.pdf: `f(n)` is the largest `k` such that for
  some `m` the number `n` divides `(m+1)...(m+k)` but no product of `k-1` of these factors. Here
  `f(t!) = t` iff `H_t` fails (a remark, not stated in Lean). They state `f(t!) = t` for `t = 15`
  and for infinitely many `t`.
* P. Erdős, C. B. Lacampagne and J. L. Selfridge, Acta Arith. 49 (1988) 507-523,
  https://www.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1988-26.pdf: if all `a_i ≤ k`, then the `a_i` are a permutation of
  `1, ..., k`, so one of them is `1`. The witnesses built here have every `a_i > 1`, hence some
  `a_i > k`, the case which that paper leaves to a later one (p. 521). No such later paper was
  found; their paper of 1993 (see "Not seen") was not read.
* H. M. Bui, S. Naprienko, K. Pratt and A. Zaharescu (BNPZ), "Binomial coefficients with divisors
  avoiding an interval", https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21221 (v3 of 30 Sep 2026), the closest published
  result: Theorem 5.1 and Proposition 5.5 give (stronger, weighted) covers for at least `K^(1-o(1))`
  of the `k ~ K`, not for all large `k`. Schinzel's conjecture is not stated there.
* Only for arbitrarily large `k`: S. Naprienko, https://github.com/slavanaprienko/erdos-387 (May
  2026, in Lean with two axioms from analytic number theory; its note proves a conjecture that T.
  Tao posted in the discussion thread, 1 Nov 2025, seen in a public copy, and that asks only for
  arbitrarily large `k`); Lean files in https://github.com/plby/lean-proofs; a note at
  https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos387.pdf.
* Every Lean file found for this variant that could be classified was a bare statement or contained
  `sorry`. Others concern finite data only, such as the counterexample `C(99215,15)`
  (https://github.com/TheJustinSunPrize/awards (JSP-000320),
  https://github.com/rjwalters/lean-genius, https://oeis.org/A294171).
  https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results lists this variant among attempted
  problems; no public proof was seen there.

## Credits for ideas

* Kummer and Chinese remainder reduction to restricted classes: Schinzel 1958, Lemma 2. Zero classes
  by default and a few moved primes: Schinzel 1958, Theorem 3, and Erdős's letter; also BNPZ.
* A prime divisor `q` of `k` in class 1, and an auxiliary prime whose class contains many large
  primes, which are thereby freed for the remaining numbers: Erdős 1957, in Schinzel 1958, pp.
  203-204. Also in Naprienko's `cover_lemma` and in BNPZ, sec. 3.2.1, which credits Erdős.
* Covering a small `d` by a large prime factor of `k - d + 1`: BNPZ v3, sec. 5.3. The bottom levels
  here are a systematic version of this move.
* Standard ingredients: Størmer's theorem (with Pell equations, after Størmer and Lehmer; Lemma 2),
  Chebyshev's 30-identity (Lemma 3), Turán's second moment method (Lemma 4), counting the prime
  factors of a binomial coefficient (Lemma 7), Rankin's bound (Lemma 10), and Mathlib's files on
  Pell's equation, Chebyshev's functions, Stirling's formula and Euler products. Lemma 11 belongs
  here too: to choose, for the numbers of an interval, primes whose powers exceed the length of the
  interval, so that no prime is chosen twice, is a standard device (known from work on products of
  consecutive integers and on Grimm's conjecture, as far as recalled; no exact source was located).
* Apparently new, produced by AI agents in this project: making this work for every large `k` that
  is not a prime power. That is the order by level with the parity argument (Lemmas 9 and 16; from a
  plan written by earlier agents of the project), Størmer's theorem for bounded levels (Lemmas 8 and
  14), the choice of `p0` for every large `k` (Lemma 5), and the counting in Lemmas 6, 12, 13 and 15
  (the weight, and the use made of Lemmas 10 and 11). None of these was found in the sources above,
  which is all that "new" means here. This file replaces a first, longer proof by the same
  agents (not included in this repository), which used Brun's sieve, power savings from Rankin sums and a
  derandomized potential. Its counting was replaced by a simpler one.

